o be safe, I'll have the script also check for node_modules and maybe run npm install if needed.\n\nNow, regarding analyzing fonts and layout from a PDF: pdfjs-dist can render the PDF page to a canvas, but to analyze fonts and layout, I might need to extract text and font information from the PDF. pdfjs-dist provides access to the PDF's internal structure, including font names and text content. I can attempt to get font sizes by measuring text or by inspecting the font matrix. Dominant colors can be analyzed from the rendered image by sampling pixels.\n\nGenerating a Marp CSS theme: Marp is a Markdown presentation tool. A Marp theme is a CSS file that defines slide size, backgrounds, colors, fonts, etc. The size should be 1000x1200 (width x height). I'll generate CSS variables for theme colors based on the dominant colors found.\n\nFor layout analysis, I can look at the positions of text elements and images from pdfjs-dist's text layer or content.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll write a script that does a reasonable approximation.\n\nBut first, I need to find the sample PDF. Its path is `C:\\Users\\mangs\\Desktop\\Content Marketing\\automated_content\\sample_slide\\1771414106196.pdf`. I suspect this file exists because it's provided. I'll have the script check for existence. If it doesn't exist, the script can error out, and I'll report that.\n\nNow, about the slide generator tool: The task says \"Use the existing slide-gen tool (openclaw-slide-generator.js)\". That suggests it's a JavaScript file that generates slides from a JSON input using Marp. I should search for it. Since my script can use Node's fs to search the filesystem, I can have it look for a file named `openclaw-slide-generator.js` in common locations: the workspace, the parent directory, maybe the OpenClaw installation directory.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tool is actually an npm package or a command-line tool like `openclaw-slide-generator`. But the name suggests it's a .js file. I'll have the script try to locate it.\n\nIf it's not found, I might need to create a minimal slide generator that reads JSON and produces a PDF via Marp. That could be a fallback.\n\nBut the task says \"existing\", so it should be there. I'll incorporate a search.\n\nGiven the exec difficulties, I'll proceed to write the script directly to the workspace using the write tool. I'll create a comprehensive script.\n\nLet me start by writing a Node.js script with all the steps. I'll break it down:\n\n1. Import required modules: fs, path, pdfjs-dist (maybe pdfjsLib), maybe canvas for image processing, though I could use pure JS for color analysis.\n2. Define constants: PDF_PATH, THEME_CSS_OUTPUT, SLIDE_GENERATOR_PATH, EXAMPLE_SLIDES_JSON, OUTPUT_PDF.\n3. Ensure node_modules exists; if not, run npm install (child_process).\n4. Check if PDF exists.\n5. Use pdfjs-dist to get the PDF document, then render first page to a canvas using pdfjs-dist's getPage and render. I'll need to get the viewport and render to a canvas. I can then get the image data.\n6. For color analysis: sample pixels from the rendered image (maybe every 10th pixel) and compute dominant colors using a simple histogram, maybe get top 3-5 colors.\n7. For font analysis: pdfjs-dist can provide text content and font info via page.getTextContent() which returns items with fontName and fontSize? Actually, textContent includes font info but not always size directly; we may need to transform. We can approximate by looking at the transformation matrix. Simpler: We can get the default appearance string which may include font size. Or we can estimate by the bounding box height. I'll try to get the font sizes from text items.\n8. For layout: We can look at the positions of text items (including images if any) to determine common regions (like header, footer, content area). We can compute margins by looking at the minX, maxX, minY, maxY of text items. Also identify if there's a title area (largest font size, centered).\n9. Generate CSS: Based on dominant colors (background, text, accent), font families (if we can extract them), slide dimensions (1000x1200). Write a .css file with Marp theme variables: `:root { --marp-theme-color-primary: ... }` etc. Actually Marp theme CSS typically uses `:root` and `section` rules. I'll look up typical Marp theme CSS structure. For example:\n\n```css\n@theme \"mytheme\";\nsection {\n  background-color: #fff;\n  color: #333;\n  font-family: \"Helvetica Neue\", Arial, sans-serif;\n  font-size: 30px;\n}\nh1, h2 {\n  color: #0066cc;\n}\n```\n\nBut we need to match the sample's style. I'll generate appropriate colors and font choices.\n\n10. Find the slide generator: Search for openclaw-slide-generator.js. I'll use a recursive search starting from the workspace up to maybe a few levels. Or maybe it's in the same directory as the workspace? I'll search from the workspace's parent directory and the user's home? But to keep it simple, I'll define a list of possible paths: `./openclaw-slide-generator.js`, `../openclaw-slide-generator.js`, `../../openclaw-slide-generator.js`, and maybe `D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\openclaw-slide-generator.js`. I'll check each.\n11. If found, read the example slides JSON (`example-openclaw-slides.json`). I'll also need to locate that file. It might be in the same folder as the generator or in the workspace. I'll search for it similarly.\n12. Use the generator: Usually such a script is run with Node, taking input JSON and output PDF path, and perhaps a theme CSS. I'll need to see its interface. If I can't run it directly, I might have to mimic its behavior. But the task says \"Use the existing slide-gen tool\", so it should be callable. I'll assume it's a Node script that accepts arguments like `--input`, `--output`, `--theme`. Or maybe it's a function I can require. I'll try to require it if it's a module, or spawn a child process.\n\nGiven the urgency, if I cannot locate the tool, I'll create a simple replacement using Marp CLI or a basic generator. But I'll first attempt to find it.\n\n13. Finally, report back: success/failure, paths to generated files (the PDF and the CSS theme), and any issues.\n\nThe script should run for up to 60 minutes, but I expect much less.\n\nI'll write the script now. I'll need to be careful with async/await, pdfjs-dist setup, and maybe using pdfjs-dist's worker.\n\nI'll also need to install canvas if needed for image manipulation, but maybe I can do without it and just use simple pixel sampling from the canvas. Canvas might be required for pdfjs-dist to render? pdfjs-dist can render to a canvas node instance. In Node, we need a canvas implementation, like the `canvas` package we installed. So we'll use that.\n\nLet's draft the script:\n\n```javascript\n// analyze-and-generate.js\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst { createCanvas, loadImage } = require('canvas');\nconst pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');\n// Or newer API: const pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js'); Actually need to check the version.\npdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js');\n\nasync function main() {\n  const PDF_PATH = 'C:\\\\Users\\\\mangs\\\\Desktop\\\\Content Marketing\\\\automated_content\\\\sample_slide\\\\1771414106196.pdf';\n  // ... other paths\n}\n```\n\nBut the pdfjs-dist API may differ. The standard way: `const pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');` for Node.js. Or use the generic `pdfjs-dist` package. I'll check the installed version later.\n\nI'll write the script step by step.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll create the script file now.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll proceed by creating a comprehensive Node.js script that handles the entire pipeline. Let me write the script to a file called `analyze-and-generate.js`."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_2de6e40fdb8e4b258b2f1a30","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"/**\n * analyze-and-generate.js\n *\n * 1. Analyzes a sample PDF to extract style characteristics (colors, fonts, layout)\n * 2. Generates a Marp CSS theme matching the sample's style\n * 3. Uses the openclaw slide generator to create a demo deck with the theme\n *\n * Run with: node analyze-and-generate.js\n */\n\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst { createCanvas } = require('canvas');\nconst pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');\n\n// Set up the PDF.js worker\npdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js');\n\n// Configuration\nconst CONFIG = {\n  // Input PDF (from the task)\n  pdfPath: 'C:\\\\Users\\\\mangs\\\\Desktop\\\\Content Marketing\\\\automated_content\\\\sample_slide\\\\1771414106196.pdf',\n\n  // Output files\n  themeCssPath: path.join(__dirname, 'generated-theme.css'),\n  outputPdfPath: path.join(__dirname, 'demo-deck.pdf'),\n\n  // Slide generator and example slides (we'll search for these)\n  slideGeneratorSearchNames: ['openclaw-slide-generator.js', 'slide-generator.js', 'generate-slides.js'],\n  exampleSlidesSearchNames: ['example-openclaw-slides.json', 'sample-slides.json', 'slides.json'],\n\n  // Slide dimensions (Marp default is 960x700, but task says 1000x1200)\n  slideWidth: 1000,\n  slideHeight: 1200,\n\n  // Color sampling parameters\n  colorSampleStep: 10, // sample every Nth pixel\n  dominantColorCount: 5\n};\n\n// Utility: read JSON safely\nfunction readJson(filePath) {\n  try {\n    const content = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');\n    return JSON.parse(content);\n  } catch (e) {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n\n// Utility: write file\nfunction writeFile(filePath, content) {\n  fs.writeFileSync(filePath, content, 'utf8');\n  console.log(`Wrote: ${filePath}`);\n}\n\n// Step 1: Check prerequisites (install deps if needed)\nfunction ensureDependencies() {\n  console.log('Checking dependencies...');\n  if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(__dirname, 'node_modules'))) {\n    console.log('node_modules not found. Running npm install...');\n    const { execSync } = require('child_process');\n    execSync('npm install pdfjs-dist canvas', { stdio: 'inherit' });\n  } else {\n    console.log('Dependencies appear to be installed.');\n  }\n}\n\n// Step 2: Check if PDF exists\nfunction checkPdfExists() {\n  if (!fs.existsSync(CONFIG.pdfPath)) {\n    throw new Error(`Sample PDF not found at: ${CONFIG.pdfPath}`);\n  }\n  console.log(`Found PDF: ${CONFIG.pdfPath}`);\n}\n\n// Step 3: Render first page to canvas and get image data\nasync function renderFirstPage() {\n  console.log('Loading PDF...');\n  const loadingTask = pdfjsLib.getDocument(CONFIG.pdfPath);\n  const pdf = await loadingTask.promise;\n  console.log(`PDF loaded. Pages: ${pdf.numPages}`);\n\n  console.log('Loading first page...');\n  const page = await pdf.getPage(1);\n\n  const viewport = page.getViewport({ scale: 1.0 }); // 1:1 for analysis\n  const canvas = createCanvas(viewport.width, viewport.height);\n  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n\n  const renderContext = {\n    canvasContext: ctx,\n    viewport: viewport\n  };\n\n  console.log('Rendering page to canvas...');\n  await page.render(renderContext).promise;\n  console.log('Render complete.');\n\n  // Get image data\n  const imageData = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);\n  return {\n    data: imageData.data,\n    width: canvas.width,\n    height: canvas.height\n  };\n}\n\n// Step 4: Analyze dominant colors\nfunction analyzeColors(imageData) {\n  const { data, width, height } = imageData;\n  const colorMap = new Map();\n\n  console.log('Sampling colors...');\n  for (let y = 0; y < height; y += CONFIG.colorSampleStep) {\n    for (let x = 0; x < width; x += CONFIG.colorSampleStep) {\n      const i = (y * width + x) * 4;\n      const r = data[i];\n      const g = data[i + 1];\n      const b = data[i + 2];\n      const a = data[i + 3];\n\n      // Skip transparent pixels (if any)\n      if (a < 128) continue;\n\n      // Quantize colors to reduce variations (e.g., round to nearest 16)\n      const qr = Math.round(r / 16) * 16;\n      const qg = Math.round(g / 16) * 16;\n      const qb = Math.round(b / 16) * 16;\n      const key = `${qr},${qg},${qb}`;\n      colorMap.set(key, (colorMap.get(key) || 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Sort by frequency\n  const sortedColors = Array.from(colorMap.entries())\n    .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])\n    .slice(0, CONFIG.dominantColorCount * 2); // Get more to filter later\n\n  // Convert to RGB and pick top ones\n  const dominant = sortedColors.slice(0, CONFIG.dominantColorCount).map(([key]) => {\n    const [r, g, b] = key.split(',').map(Number);\n    return { r, g, b };\n  });\n\n  console.log(`Found ${dominant.length} dominant colors.`);\n  return dominant;\n}\n\n// Step 5: Analyze fonts and layout\nasync function analyzeLayout() {\n  console.log('Analyzing layout and fonts...');\n  const loadingTask = pdfjsLib.getDocument(CONFIG.pdfPath);\n  const pdf = await loadingTask.promise;\n  const page = await pdf.getPage(1);\n  const textContent = await page.getTextContent();\n\n  const items = textContent.items; // Array of text items with transform, fontName, fontSize, etc.\n\n  // Collect font sizes and names\n  const fontSizes = new Set();\n  const fontNames = new Set();\n  const positions = [];\n\n  items.forEach(item => {\n    // Font size may be in item.transform or item.fontSize? Usually pdf.js gives transform matrix.\n    // We can estimate font size from the transformation: the scaling factor in Y (transform[3]?).\n    // Actually, item.transform is an array [scaleX, skewY, skewX, scaleY, translateX, translateY].\n    // The font size approximate: scaleY * something? But there's also item.hasEval? We'll use item.fontSize if present.\n    if (item.fontSize) {\n      fontSizes.add(Math.round(item.fontSize));\n    } else if (item.transform) {\n      // Approximate: scaleY is the vertical scaling; actual glyph size depends on font units.\n      // We can also compute bounding box height: (item.transform[3] * defaultFontSize?) Hard.\n      // For simplicity, just record that font exists.\n    }\n    if (item.fontName) {\n      fontNames.add(item.fontName);\n    }\n    // Get the item's bounding box (item.transform gives the text matrix; we can compute the bbox from item.width and item.height? Or from item.transform and item.width.)\n    // pdf.js textContent items often have transform and width. The bbox can be computed as:\n    // x = transform[4], y = transform[5], width = item.width, height = item.height? Not always.\n    // For layout we care more about positions: we'll collect x,y of baseline.\n    let x = item.transform ? item.transform[4] : 0;\n    let y = item.transform ? item.transform[5] : 0;\n    // item.width might be present; height maybe derived.\n    positions.push({ x, y, fontSize: item.fontSize || null, fontName: item.fontName || null });\n  });\n\n  // Determine margins: min x, max x, min y, max y of text items\n  if (positions.length > 0) {\n    const xs = positions.map(p => p.x);\n    const ys = positions.map(p => p.y);\n    const minX = Math.min(...xs);\n    const maxX = Math.max(...xs);\n    const minY = Math.min(...ys);\n    const maxY = Math.max(...ys);\n\n    // The page viewport dimensions\n    const viewport = page.getViewport({ scale: 1 });\n    const pageWidth = viewport.width;\n    const pageHeight = viewport.height;\n\n    const margins = {\n      left: minX,\n      right: pageWidth - maxX,\n      top: pageHeight - maxY, // Note: PDF coordinate origin is bottom-left usually, but pdf.js viewport top is 0? Actually page.getViewport returns height but transform y might be from bottom. Need to be careful. But for margin estimates approximate, we can compute top as minY? Let's recalc.\n    };\n\n    // Actually in PDF coordinate system, origin is bottom-left. pdf.js transforms to viewport coordinates where (0,0) is top-left? Not exactly. The textContent items have y as distance from bottom? Let's check viewport: we can convert using page.getViewport. For simplicity, we'll treat minY as bottom margin, maxY as top? I'll compute margins differently:\n    // We'll compute the spread: assume the text block covers from minY to maxY. The top margin = pageHeight - maxY, bottom margin = minY.\n    margins.top = pageHeight - maxY;\n    margins.bottom = minY;\n    // Left/right already computed\n    console.log(`Estimated margins: left=${margins.left.toFixed(1)}, right=${margins.right.toFixed(1)}, top=${margins.top.toFixed(1)}, bottom=${margins.bottom.toFixed(1)}`);\n  }\n\n  // Determine typical font size: median of sizes\n  const sizesArray = Array.from(fontSizes).filter(s => s > 0);\n  let typicalFontSize = 24; // default fallback\n  if (sizesArray.length > 0) {\n    sizesArray.sort((a, b) => a - b);\n    const mid = Math.floor(sizesArray.length / 2);\n    typicalFontSize = sizesArray[mid];\n  }\n  console.log(`Font sizes found: ${sizesArray.join(', ')}. Typical: ${typicalFontSize}`);\n\n  // Font family: We'll pick a common one from the set, or default to sans-serif.\n  // pdf.js fontName often includes style like \"Helvetica-Bold\". We'll strip style to get family.\n  let primaryFontFamily = 'sans-serif';\n  if (fontNames.size > 0) {\n    // Pick the most frequent font name if we had counts, but we just have set. So take first.\n    const rawFont = fontNames.values().next().value;\n    // Remove style suffix after dash if present\n    primaryFontFamily = rawFont.split('-')[0].replace(/^[\"']+|[\"']+$/g, '');\n  }\n  console.log(`Primary font family: ${primaryFontFamily}`);\n\n  return {\n    typicalFontSize,\n    primaryFontFamily,\n    margins: positions.length > 0 ? { left: minX, right: pageWidth - maxX, top: pageHeight - maxY, bottom: minY } : null,\n    textCount: items.length\n  };\n}\n\n// Step 6: Generate Marp CSS theme\nfunction generateTheme(dominantColors, layoutInfo) {\n  console.log('Generating Marp CSS theme...');\n\n  // Heuristic: The most frequent color is likely background or text.\n  // We can sample: if the top color is light (high luminance), assume it's background; if dark, assume text.\n  // Compute luminance: 0.299*R + 0.587*G + 0.114*B\n  const getLuminance = (c) => 0.299 * c.r + 0.587 * c.g + 0.114 * c.b;\n\n  // Map colors to hex\n  const toHex = (c) => '#' + [c.r, c.g, c.b].map(x => x.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n\n  // Sort by frequency (already sorted most frequent first)\n  const topColor = dominantColors[0];\n  const secondColor = dominantColors[1];\n  const thirdColor = dominantColors[2];\n\n  // Determine if background is light or dark\n  const topLum = getLuminance(topColor);\n  let backgroundColor, textColor, accentColor, secondaryColor;\n\n  if (topLum > 128) {\n    // Light background\n    backgroundColor = toHex(topColor);\n    textColor = '#222222'; // dark text\n    accentColor = secondColor ? toHex(secondColor) : '#0066cc';\n    secondaryColor = thirdColor ? toHex(thirdColor) : '#ff6600';\n  } else {\n    // Dark background\n    backgroundColor = toHex(topColor);\n    textColor = '#eeeeee'; // light text\n    accentColor = secondColor ? toHex(secondColor) : '#4da6ff';\n    secondaryColor = thirdColor ? toHex(thirdColor) : '#ff9933';\n  }\n\n  // Font size scaling: We need to map the PDF's typical font size to slide CSS font size.\n  // The slide dimensions are 1000x1200. The PDF's font size in points might need scaling.\n  // Approximate: target slide font size ~ 32px for body. We'll compute ratio based on typicalFontSize from PDF (which might be in points, 1pt = 1/72 inch, but at 96 DPI screen maybe 1.333px per pt). It's messy.\n  // Simpler: Set body font size to something like 28px; then headers scale relative.\n  const baseFontSize = Math.round(layoutInfo.typicalFontSize * 0.7); // rough scaling to slide\n  const headerScale = 1.8; // h1 relative to body\n\n  // Build CSS string\n  const css = `@theme \"custom-from-pdf\";\n/* Generated by analyze-and-generate.js */\nsection {\n  width: ${CONFIG.slideWidth}px;\n  height: ${CONFIG.slideHeight}px;\n  background-color: ${backgroundColor};\n  color: ${textColor};\n  font-family: \"${layoutInfo.primaryFontFamily}\", sans-serif;\n  font-size: ${baseFontSize}px;\n  line-height: 1.5;\n  padding: ${Math.round(layoutInfo.margins ? layoutInfo.margins.left * 0.5 : 50)}px ${Math.round(layoutInfo.margins ? layoutInfo.margins.right * 0.5 : 50)}px;\n}\n\n/* Headings */\nh1, h2, h3 {\n  color: ${accentColor};\n  font-weight: bold;\n}\nh1 {\n  font-size: ${Math.round(baseFontSize * headerScale)}px;\n  margin-bottom: 0.5em;\n}\nh2 {\n  font-size: ${Math.round(baseFontSize * headerScale * 0.85)}px;\n}\nh3 {\n  font-size: ${Math.round(baseFontSize * headerScale * 0.7)}px;\n}\n\n/* Links and accents */\na {\n  color: ${accentColor};\n}\n\n/* Use secondary color for highlights, blockquotes, etc. */\nmark, blockquote {\n  background-color: ${secondaryColor}33; /* 20% opacity */\n  border-left: 4px solid ${secondaryColor};\n  padding: 0.2em 0.5em;\n}\n`;\n\n  writeFile(CONFIG.themeCssPath, css);\n  console.log('Theme CSS generated.');\n}\n\n// Step 7: Find slide generator and example slides\nfunction findFile(searchNames, startDir = __dirname) {\n  // Search upwards and in some subfolders\n  let dir = startDir;\n  const maxDepth = 5;\n  for (let depth = 0; depth < maxDepth; depth++) {\n    for (const name of searchNames) {\n      const candidate = path.join(dir, name);\n      if (fs.existsSync(candidate)) {\n        return candidate;\n      }\n    }\n    // Move up one level\n    const parent = path.dirname(dir);\n    if (parent === dir) break;\n    dir = parent;\n  }\n  // Also search in a sibling 'tools' folder?\n  // Maybe also check the OpenClaw root?\n  const openclawRoot = path.resolve(__dirname, '..', '..', '..');\n  if (fs.existsSync(openclawRoot)) {\n    for (const name of searchNames) {\n      const candidate = path.join(openclawRoot, name);\n      if (fs.existsSync(candidate)) {\n        return candidate;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nfunction locateResources() {\n  console.log('Locating slide generator and example slides...');\n  const generatorPath = findFile(CONFIG.slideGeneratorSearchNames);\n  const exampleSlidesPath = findFile(CONFIG.exampleSlidesSearchNames);\n\n  if (!generatorPath) {\n    throw new Error('Could not find openclaw-slide-generator.js. Please ensure it is in the workspace or a parent directory.');\n  }\n  if (!exampleSlidesPath) {\n    throw new Error('Could not find example-openclaw-slides.json.');\n  }\n  console.log(`Found generator: ${generatorPath}`);\n  console.log(`Found example slides: ${exampleSlidesPath}`);\n  return { generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath };\n}\n\n// Step 8: Use the slide generator to create the demo deck\nfunction runSlideGenerator(generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath, themePath, outputPdfPath) {\n  console.log('Running slide generator...');\n  // The generator likely expects to be run with Node and accepts arguments.\n  // Try to require it if it's a module, otherwise spawn.\n  try {\n    // Attempt to require the generator (if it exports a function)\n    const generator = require(generatorPath);\n    if (typeof generator.generate === 'function') {\n      // Assume it takes input JSON, theme CSS, output PDF path\n      const slides = readJson(exampleSlidesPath);\n      generator.generate(slides, themePath, outputPdfPath);\n      console.log('Slide generation via require() succeeded.');\n      return;\n    } else {\n      throw new Error('Generator does not export generate function.');\n    }\n  } catch (e) {\n    // Fallback: spawn a child process\n    console.log(`Spawning generator as script: node ${generatorPath} ...`);\n    const { spawnSync } = require('child_process');\n    const args = [generatorPath, '--input', exampleSlidesPath, '--theme', themePath, '--output', outputPdfPath];\n    const result = spawnSync('node', args, { stdio: 'inherit' });\n    if (result.status !== 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Slide generator failed with exit code ${result.status}`);\n    }\n    console.log('Slide generation via spawn succeeded.');\n  }\n}\n\n// Main async function\nasync function main() {\n  console.log('=== Starting PDF analysis and slide generation ===');\n  try {\n    ensureDependencies();\n    checkPdfExists();\n\n    // Render and analyze\n    const imageData = await renderFirstPage();\n    const dominantColors = analyzeColors(imageData);\n    console.log('Dominant colors:', dominantColors.map(c => `rgb(${c.r},${c.g},${c.b})`).join(', '));\n\n    const layoutInfo = await analyzeLayout();\n    console.log('Layout info:', JSON.stringify(layoutInfo, null, 2));\n\n    // Generate theme\n    generateTheme(dominantColors, layoutInfo);\n\n    // Locate generator and example slides\n    const { generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath } = locateResources();\n\n    // Run generator\n    runSlideGenerator(generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath, CONFIG.themeCssPath, CONFIG.outputPdfPath);\n\n    console.log('=== Completed successfully ===');\n    console.log(`Theme CSS: ${CONFIG.themeCssPath}`);\n    console.log(`Output PDF: ${CONFIG.outputPdfPath}`);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('ERROR:', err.message);\n    console.error(err.stack);\n    process.exit(1);\n  }\n}\n\nmain();\n","file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\workspace-social\\analyze-and-generate.js"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":22133,"output":9252,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":31385,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771418805140}}
