e-gyp ERR! stack     at ChildProcess.<anonymous> (C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\openclaw_node_modules\\node_modules\\@mapbox\\node-pre-gyp\\lib\\util\\compile.js:89:23)\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! stack     at ChildProcess.emit (node:events:508:28)\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! stack     at maybeClose (node:internal/child_process:1101:16)\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! stack     at ChildProcess._handle.onexit (node:internal/child_process:305:5)\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! System Windows_NT 10.0.26200\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! command \"D:\\\\Software\\\\node\\\\node.exe\" \"C:\\\\Users\\\\mangs\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Temp\\\\openclaw_node_modules\\\\node_modules\\\\@mapbox\\\\node-pre-gyp\\\\bin\\\\node-pre-gyp\" \"install\" \"--fallback-to-build\" \"--update-binary\"\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! cwd C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\openclaw_node_modules\\node_modules\\canvas\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! node -v v24.12.0\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! node-pre-gyp -v v1.0.11\nnpm error node-pre-gyp ERR! not ok\nnpm error A complete log of this run can be found in: C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\npm-cache\\_logs\\2026-02-18T12_58_15_070Z-debug-0.log\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1771419523693}}
{"type":"message","id":"013f5837","parentId":"20eb817b","timestamp":"2026-02-18T12:59:29.799Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The error is clear: canvas build failed because it cannot find cairo.h and other dependencies. Also, prebuilt binary for canvas@2.11.2 with Node 24 (node-v137) is not available (404). So building from source fails due to missing GTK libraries (cairo, pango, etc). This is typical on Windows without installing GTK manually.\n\nThus, canvas installation is not going to succeed in this environment. We need a simpler approach that does not require canvas.\n\nThe task says: \"canvas may require build tools; if it fails, we'll use a simpler approach.\" So we need to implement a fallback that doesn't use canvas. How else can we render PDF to an image without canvas? We could use an external tool like ImageMagick, Ghostscript, or a command-line converter. But those might not be installed either.\n\nMaybe we can use pdfjs-dist's built-in text and vector extraction to approximate colors without rasterizing? For dominant colors, we could sample the colors from the PDF's fill color operators in the content streams. That's more complex but possible using pdfjs-dist's `page.getOperatorList()` which returns a list of drawing operations with arguments. We could iterate through the operators, looking at `op` values like 'fi' (fill), 'f' (fill path), 'rg' (set fill color RGB), etc. We could track the current fill color and sample it along with coordinates? But we'd still need to rasterize to know which colors appear on the page? Actually, we could collect all colors used in the content stream (both stroke and fill) and also possibly from images (there are image operators). We could also count frequency of each color based on the amount of area painted? That's hard.\n\nAlternatively, we could use a different library that can render PDF to a PNG without native dependencies, like `pdf2pic` uses `pdfjs-dist` and `canvas` too. `pdf2image` uses `pdftoppm` which is part of Poppler. If Poppler is installed, we could use that. But likely not.\n\nAnother idea: Use `puppeteer` to render PDF to image using headless Chrome. That would require installing puppeteer and a Chromium binary, which is large but might be doable via npm and would download a binary. However, that might be heavy and also might have dependencies.\n\nGiven the constraints, maybe the \"simpler approach\" refers to not using canvas at all but instead using a predefined theme if analysis fails. But the task says to analyze the sample PDF and generate a theme that matches. That implies we must get colors from the PDF. Could we extract colors from the PDF without rendering? Possibly by reading the page's resources and content streams and parsing the graphics state changes. That is non-trivial but maybe doable with pdfjs-dist's API: `page.getTextContent()` for text, and `page.getAnnotations()` for annotations. But for vector graphics, there is `page.getOperatorList()` which gives low-level operators. We could simulate the painting to some extent: track transformation matrix, clipping, and fill colors. But to get \"dominant colors\" meaning colors that cover large areas, we would need to compute the area of each filled shape. That's complex.\n\nMaybe the intended simpler approach is to use the PDF's metadata or XMP? Not likely.\n\nGiven the time, perhaps I should attempt to use a pure JS solution for PDF rendering that doesn't require native compilation: `pdfjs-dist` itself is pure JS, but its renderer expects a canvas. However, there is a node-canvas implementation called `canvas` which is native. Is there a pure JS canvas implementation? There is `node-skia-canvas`? That's also native. There's `jsdom` and `canvas` from `canvas` package still native. There's `no-canvas`? Not.\n\nMaybe we can use `sharp` to manipulate images but not render PDF.\n\nAnother thought: Could we convert the PDF to an image using an online service? Not allowed.\n\nPerhaps we can cheat: Since we only need to generate a theme, we could assume typical colors based on common slide templates? That wouldn't be matching the sample.\n\nWait, maybe we can use `pdfjs-dist` to get the page as a JPEG via `page.getViewport` and then use `node-fetch` to call an external API? No.\n\nGiven the environment, the simplest may be to use the `gm` (GraphicsMagick) or `imagemagick` command-line if available. But not guaranteed.\n\nMaybe the failure is due to Node 24 being too new; an older Node version might have prebuilt canvas binaries. If we could use a Node version that has prebuilt canvas, we could avoid compiling. But we are stuck with Node 24 from the runtime.\n\nMaybe we can try to install an older version of Node on the fly? Not possible.\n\nPerhaps we can use the `canvas` version 2.11.2 but from a different prebuilt binary site? The error shows it tried to download from GitHub releases for node-v137 (Node 24). That binary doesn't exist. If we could force it to use the binary for Node 20 or 22, maybe it would work? Not safe.\n\nMaybe we can use the `canvas` npm package that includes prebuilt binaries for Node 20 LTS; we could install canvas@2.11.2 but with a different node ABI? Not possible.\n\nGiven the dead end, I must consider that the intended solution may be to use a different approach that doesn't require canvas at all. Perhaps the script should not render to an image; instead it should directly analyze PDF content for colors using pdfjs-dist's `page.getTextContent` and `page.getAnnotations` and maybe `page.getOperatorList()` but not require canvas. But then how to get dominant colors? Could rely on the PDF's `/Resources` color spaces? Possibly but complex.\n\nMaybe the sample PDF is simple with a solid background and text, and we can infer the background color by assuming the most common color is white and text color is dark. But that's not robust.\n\nWait, the task says: \"renders the first page to an image using pdfjs-dist, and analyzes dominant colors, approximate font sizes, and layout.\" So rendering is explicit. So we must render.\n\nGiven that canvas is impossible to install on this Windows gateway (due to missing GTK), we need a different rendering engine that doesn't have native dependencies. One option is to use `pdfjs-dist` with a pure JS implementation of the Canvas API like `canvas` from the `node-canvas` package is the only one. But maybe we can use `jsdom` and `canvas` from `canvas` still native.\n\nWhat about using `puppeteer`? It downloads Chromium and uses it to render. That would also require a lot of download but doesn't need native compilation. However, it might be allowed? The task says \"canvas may require build tools; if it fails, we'll use a simpler approach.\" That suggests they anticipate canvas failure and want us to use something else that is simpler. Maybe \"simpler approach\" means using the `openclaw-slide-generator.js` tool directly without custom theme? But that wouldn't match sample.\n\nMaybe I misinterpreted: The task might be from the main agent to me (subagent) and they want me to do these steps. The note \"if it fails, we'll use a simpler approach\" could be a note from the main agent to themselves, not to me. But it's in the instruction. I think it means that if canvas installation fails, I should use a simpler approach (like not using pdfjs-dist to render, but maybe manually specify a theme based on common defaults). But that would not be fulfilling the requirement.\n\nLet's read the instruction again: \"1) Install Node.js dependencies: npm install pdfjs-dist canvas (canvas may require build tools; if it fails, we'll use a simpler approach).\"\n\nInterpretation: I should attempt to install pdfjs-dist and canvas. If canvas fails, then I should use a simpler approach (maybe a different library or a headless browser?). But they didn't specify what the simpler approach is. It might be to use `pdfjs-dist` with a different canvas implementation? Or perhaps use `canvas` from a prebuilt binary if available, else fallback to using `openclaw-slide-generator` with a default theme without analysis. But that wouldn't match.\n\nGiven the futility, maybe I should give up on canvas and try a different route: Use the openclaw slide generator to generate a PDF from the example slides with a default theme, and then skip the analysis part. But that wouldn't satisfy the task's requirement to generate a theme that matches the sample's style.\n\nPerhaps I can analyze the PDF without rendering by using pdfjs-dist's `page.getTextContent` to get font info and `page.getAnnotations` for images, and maybe `page.getOperatorList` for colors. That would be a fallback approach that doesn't need canvas. I could implement that as a fallback when canvas is not available. But the task explicitly says \"renders the first page to an image using pdfjs-dist\". That implies we must use pdfjs-dist's rendering capability, which requires a canvas.\n\nBut maybe pdfjs-dist has a built-in renderer that can output to a PDF or SVG? There is `page.render` that takes a `CanvasRenderingContext2D`. Without canvas, I can't get pixel data.\n\nIs there a way to use `pdfjs-dist` in a headless browser environment like jsdom with a canvas implementation that is pure JS? There is a package `canvas` that uses Cairo, that's native. There is `node-canvas-prebuilt` which includes prebuilt binaries for many platforms. Could we try that? `npm install canvas-prebuilt`? That might have prebuilt binaries for Node 24? Not sure.\n\nMaybe the problem is that we are using the gateway, which runs as a service without desktop interaction, and the installer for canvas can't find the GTK libraries. Could we install the GTK dependencies? Not feasible.\n\nGiven the time, I might try to see if we can succeed with canvas by using a version that has prebuilt binaries for Node 24. The error indicates that the prebuilt binary for canvas@2.11.2 for node-v137 (Node 24) is missing. Maybe canvas 2.11.2 doesn't support Node 24, but a newer version like canvas 2.12.x might. Let's check the canvas release notes. Canvas 2.11.2 is the latest stable as of early 2024. Node 24 is very new (2025). So likely no prebuilt yet. Could try canvas@3.0.0 (alpha) maybe? But not sure.\n\nAlternatively, we could downgrade Node version? Not possible.\n\nMaybe we can avoid canvas entirely by using a different approach: Use `pdf2pic` with `pdftoppm`? That requires poppler.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe the test environment is expected to have canvas already installed globally. Could it be that the gateway already has canvas installed globally? My earlier try to require('canvas') failed because it looked in the local node_modules, but if I set NODE_PATH to global node_modules, maybe it would work. But we can check if canvas is installed globally by running `npm list -g canvas`. Not easy.\n\nLet's think differently: The subagent is expected to complete the task within 60 minutes. We've spent a lot of time on dependency issues. Perhaps I should try a completely different strategy: Instead of writing a script that installs dependencies, use the fact that the main agent likely already has a working environment with pdfjs-dist and canvas installed globally. Could I just use them directly without installing? The script failed because it couldn't find canvas. But maybe if I set NODE_PATH to include the global node_modules, it might find it. The global node_modules is typically at `C:\\Users\\[user]\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm\\node_modules` on Windows. I could add that to NODE_PATH before requiring.\n\nLet's try: In ensureDependencies, before trying require, set process.env.NODE_PATH to include the global node_modules path. But it's better to check if canvas is available globally. However, we don't know the user.\n\nMaybe the gateway already has a node_modules in the workspace? But earlier we saw no node_modules.\n\nWhat if I just try to require('canvas') and if it fails, I try to require from a relative path like '../node_modules'? Not.\n\nGiven the time, I'm going to pivot: The task might be easier if I don't rely on canvas at all. Could I maybe use the `openclaw-slide-generator.js` tool to generate a theme directly from the PDF? That seems unlikely.\n\nWait, maybe I misread: The task says \"Write a script that reads the sample PDF at ..., renders the first page to an image using pdfjs-dist, and analyzes dominant colors, approximate font sizes, and layout.\" That is the script I'm writing. So I must write that script. The canvas is a dependency for that script. If canvas fails, the script can't run. But the note \"if it fails, we'll use a simpler approach\" might refer to us (the main team) having a fallback plan if canvas can't be installed on the server; maybe they'll use a different machine. As a subagent, I just need to attempt the installation and report issues. My job is to complete the task to the best of my ability given the environment.\n\nGiven that the gateway environment lacks the build tools for canvas, I could try to install the GTK dependencies first? Not practical.\n\nMaybe I can use the `pdfjs-dist` package's `getDocument` and then `page.getViewport` and `page.render` with a custom canvas implementation that is a stub? But we need pixel data.\n\nAnother idea: Use `pdfjs-dist` to extract the page as a JPEG via `page.render` to a `NodeCanvas` from the `canvas` package. That's what we did.\n\nGiven the repeated failure, maybe I should try to use a prebuilt binary of canvas by installing the `canvas-prebuilt` package instead of `canvas`. There is a package `canvas-prebuilt` that used to provide prebuilt binaries. But it might be deprecated. Could try: `npm install canvas-prebuilt`. However, that package might still require some dependencies.\n\nAlternatively, use `@napi-rs/canvas` which is a Rust-based canvas implementation with prebuilt binaries for many Node versions. That might work. Let's check: There is a package `@napi-rs/canvas` that provides a NodeCanvas implementation with prebuilt binaries for many platforms. It's a drop-in replacement for the `canvas` API? Possibly yes. The API is similar but not identical. Our code uses `createCanvas` from `canvas`. `@napi-rs/canvas` also exports `createCanvas`. So we could try to install that instead of `canvas`. The package name is `@napi-rs/canvas`. We can require it as `require('@napi-rs/canvas')`. It might work with pdfjs-dist because it just needs a canvas context.\n\nLet's try that: Change the dependency to `@napi-rs/canvas` instead of `canvas`. And keep pdfjs-dist.\n\nModify ensureDependencies to install `@napi-rs/canvas` (maybe version that supports Node 24). The package provides prebuilt binaries for many Node versions via napi. That would avoid native build.\n\nWe can try: `npm install --prefix installDir pdfjs-dist@2.16.105 @napi-rs/canvas`.\n\nThen in loadExternalModules, we require `@napi-rs/canvas` and use its createCanvas.\n\nBut pdfjs-dist's render expects a canvas with a context that matches the HTMLCanvasElement interface. `@napi-rs/canvas` provides a compatible implementation. Should work.\n\nLet's do that.\n\nI'll modify the script accordingly.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"After reviewing the logs, canvas fails to build because it lacks GTK dependencies. I'll switch to `@napi-rs/canvas`, which provides prebuilt binaries via N-API and should work with Node 24 without build tools. I'll also keep pdfjs-dist at a compatible version. Let's update the script to use this alternative:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0e4b930907c341008351a284","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"/**\n * analyze-and-generate.js\n *\n * Analyzes a sample PDF to extract style characteristics (colors, fonts, layout).\n * Generates a Marp CSS theme and uses the openclaw slide generator to create a demo deck.\n * Outputs theme CSS and PDF as base64 via stdout for capture.\n */\n\n// Debug\nconsole.error(`[DEBUG] Script started at ${new Date().toISOString()}`);\n\nimport { createRequire } from 'module';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'url';\nconst require = createRequire(import.meta.url);\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst { spawnSync } = require('child_process');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst os = require('os');\n\n// Derive __dirname\nconst __filename = fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);\nconst __dirname = path.dirname(__filename);\n\n// Use temp directory for files to avoid permission issues\nconst TEMP_DIR = os.tmpdir();\n\nlet NODE_INSTALL_DIR = null;\n\nconst CONFIG = {\n  pdfPath: 'C:\\\\Users\\\\mangs\\\\Desktop\\\\Content Marketing\\\\automated_content\\\\sample_slide\\1771414106196.pdf',\n  themeCssPath: path.join(TEMP_DIR, 'openclaw-theme.css'),\n  outputPdfPath: path.join(TEMP_DIR, 'openclaw-demo.pdf'),\n  slideGeneratorSearchNames: ['openclaw-slide-generator.js', 'slide-generator.js', 'generate-slides.js'],\n  exampleSlidesSearchNames: ['example-openclaw-slides.json', 'sample-slides.json', 'slides.json'],\n  workspaceRoot: path.resolve(__dirname, '..', '..', '..'), // up to openclaw root\n  slideWidth: 1000,\n  slideHeight: 1200,\n  colorSampleStep: 10,\n  dominantColorCount: 5\n};\n\n// Utility: read JSON\nfunction readJson(filePath) {\n  try {\n    return JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8'));\n  } catch (e) {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n\n// Utility: write file\nfunction writeFile(filePath, content) {\n  fs.writeFileSync(filePath, 'utf8', content);\n  console.log(`Wrote file: ${filePath}`);\n}\n\n// Ensure dependencies\nfunction ensureDependencies() {\n  console.log('Checking dependencies...');\n  try {\n    // Try to require either canvas or @napi-rs/canvas\n    try {\n      require('@napi-rs/canvas');\n      console.log('@napi-rs/canvas already available.');\n    } catch (e) {\n      require('canvas');\n      console.log('canvas already available.');\n    }\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.log('No canvas implementation found. Installing dependencies to temp dir...');\n    const installDir = path.join(TEMP_DIR, 'openclaw_node_modules');\n    if (!fs.existsSync(installDir)) fs.mkdirSync(installDir, { recursive: true });\n\n    // Use npm with shell option\n    const args = ['install', '--prefix', installDir, 'pdfjs-dist@2.16.105', '@napi-rs/canvas'];\n    const result = spawnSync('npm', args, { cwd: installDir, stdio: 'pipe', encoding: 'utf-8', shell: true });\n    const logPath = path.join(TEMP_DIR, 'npm-install.log');\n    if (result.stdout) fs.writeFileSync(logPath, result.stdout);\n    if (result.stderr) fs.appendFileSync(logPath, result.stderr);\n    if (result.status !== 0) {\n      throw new Error(`npm install failed with code ${result.status}. See ${logPath}`);\n    }\n    NODE_INSTALL_DIR = installDir;\n    process.env.NODE_PATH = path.join(installDir, 'node_modules');\n    const Module = require('module');\n    Module._initPaths();\n    console.log('Dependencies installed and module paths updated.');\n  }\n}\n\n// Load external modules\nlet createCanvas = null;\nlet pdfjsLib = null;\nfunction loadExternalModules() {\n  // Try to load canvas implementation: prefer @napi-rs/canvas, fallback to canvas\n  let canvasModule;\n  try {\n    canvasModule = require('@napi-rs/canvas');\n    console.log('Using @napi-rs/canvas.');\n  } catch (e) {\n    canvasModule = require('canvas');\n    console.log('Using canvas.');\n  }\n  createCanvas = canvasModule.createCanvas;\n  // pdfjs-dist\n  pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');\n  pdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js');\n  console.log('External modules loaded.');\n}\n\n// Check PDF exists\nfunction checkPdfExists() {\n  if (!fs.existsSync(CONFIG.pdfPath)) {\n    throw new Error(`Sample PDF not found at: ${CONFIG.pdfPath}`);\n  }\n  console.log(`PDF found: ${CONFIG.pdfPath}`);\n}\n\n// Render first page\nasync function renderFirstPage() {\n  console.log('Loading PDF...');\n  const loadingTask = pdfjsLib.getDocument(CONFIG.pdfPath);\n  const pdf = await loadingTask.promise;\n  console.log(`PDF loaded: ${pdf.numPages} pages.`);\n  const page = await pdf.getPage(1);\n  const viewport = page.getViewport({ scale: 1.0 });\n  const canvas = createCanvas(viewport.width, viewport.height);\n  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n  await page.render({ canvasContext: ctx, viewport }).promise;\n  console.log('First page rendered to canvas.');\n  const imageData = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);\n  return { data: imageData.data, width: canvas.width, height: canvas.height };\n}\n\n// Analyze colors (same)\nfunction analyzeColors(imageData) {\n  const { data, width, height } = imageData;\n  const colorMap = new Map();\n  console.log('Sampling colors...');\n  for (let y = 0; y < height; y += CONFIG.colorSampleStep) {\n    for (let x = 0; x < width; x += CONFIG.colorSampleStep) {\n      const i = (y * width + x) * 4;\n      const r = data[i], g = data[i+1], b = data[i+2], a = data[i+3];\n      if (a < 128) continue;\n      const qr = Math.round(r / 16) * 16;\n      const qg = Math.round(g / 16) * 16;\n      const qb = Math.round(b / 16) * 16;\n      const key = `${qr},${qg},${qb}`;\n      colorMap.set(key, (colorMap.get(key) || 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n  const sorted = Array.from(colorMap.entries())\n    .sort((a,b) => b[1] - a[1])\n    .slice(0, CONFIG.dominantColorCount * 2);\n  const dominant = sorted.slice(0, CONFIG.dominantColorCount).map(([key]) => {\n    const [r,g,b] = key.split(',').map(Number);\n    return { r, g, b };\n  });\n  console.log(`Found ${dominant.length} dominant colors.`);\n  return dominant;\n}\n\n// Analyze layout (same)\nasync function analyzeLayout() {\n  console.log('Analyzing layout and fonts...');\n  const loadingTask = pdfjsLib.getDocument(CONFIG.pdfPath);\n  const pdf = await loadingTask.promise;\n  const page = await pdf.getPage(1);\n  const textContent = await page.getTextContent();\n  const items = textContent.items;\n\n  const fontSizes = new Set();\n  const fontNames = new Set();\n  const positions = [];\n\n  items.forEach(item => {\n    if (item.fontSize) fontSizes.add(Math.round(item.fontSize));\n    if (item.fontName) fontNames.add(item.fontName);\n    const x = item.transform ? item.transform[4] : 0;\n    const y = item.transform ? item.transform[5] : 0;\n    positions.push({ x, y, fontSize: item.fontSize || null, fontName: item.fontName || null });\n  });\n\n  let typicalFontSize = 24;\n  const sizesArray = Array.from(fontSizes).filter(s => s > 0);\n  if (sizesArray.length > 0) {\n    sizesArray.sort((a,b) => a-b);\n    typicalFontSize = sizesArray[Math.floor(sizesArray.length/2)];\n  }\n\n  let primaryFontFamily = 'sans-serif';\n  if (fontNames.size > 0) {\n    const rawFont = fontNames.values().next().value;\n    primaryFontFamily = rawFont.split('-')[0].replace(/^[\"']+|[\"']+$/g, '');\n  }\n\n  let margins = null;\n  if (positions.length > 0) {\n    const xs = positions.map(p => p.x);\n    const ys = positions.map(p => p.y);\n    const minX = Math.min(...xs), maxX = Math.max(...xs);\n    const minY = Math.min(...ys), maxY = Math.max(...ys);\n    const viewport = page.getViewport({ scale: 1 });\n    const pageWidth = viewport.width, pageHeight = viewport.height;\n    margins = { left: minX, right: pageWidth - maxX, top: pageHeight - maxY, bottom: minY };\n    console.log(`Margins: left=${margins.left.toFixed(1)}, right=${margins.right.toFixed(1)}, top=${margins.top.toFixed(1)}, bottom=${margins.bottom.toFixed(1)}`);\n  }\n\n  console.log(`Font sizes: ${sizesArray.join(', ')}. Typical: ${typicalFontSize}`);\n  console.log(`Primary font: ${primaryFontFamily}`);\n\n  return { typicalFontSize, primaryFontFamily, margins, textCount: items.length };\n}\n\n// Generate CSS theme (same)\nfunction generateTheme(dominantColors, layoutInfo) {\n  console.log('Generating theme CSS...');\n  const getLuminance = c => 0.299*c.r + 0.587*c.g + 0.114*c.b;\n  const toHex = c => '#' + [c.r, c.g, c.b].map(x => x.toString(16).padStart(2,'0')).join('');\n\n  const topColor = dominantColors[0];\n  const secondColor = dominantColors[1];\n  const thirdColor = dominantColors[2];\n  const topLum = getLuminance(topColor);\n\n  let backgroundColor, textColor, accentColor, secondaryColor;\n  if (topLum > 128) {\n    backgroundColor = toHex(topColor);\n    textColor = '#222222';\n    accentColor = secondColor ? toHex(secondColor) : '#0066cc';\n    secondaryColor = thirdColor ? toHex(thirdColor) : '#ff6600';\n  } else {\n    backgroundColor = toHex(topColor);\n    textColor = '#eeeeee';\n    accentColor = secondColor ? toHex(secondColor) : '#4da6ff';\n    secondaryColor = thirdColor ? toHex(thirdColor) : '#ff9933';\n  }\n\n  const baseFontSize = Math.round(layoutInfo.typicalFontSize * 0.7);\n  const headerScale = 1.8;\n  const padH = Math.round(layoutInfo.margins ? layoutInfo.margins.left * 0.5 : 50);\n  const padV = Math.round(layoutInfo.margins ? layoutInfo.margins.right * 0.5 : 50);\n\n  const css = `@theme \"custom-from-pdf\";\nsection {\n  width: ${CONFIG.slideWidth}px;\n  height: ${CONFIG.slideHeight}px;\n  background-color: ${backgroundColor};\n  color: ${textColor};\n  font-family: \"${layoutInfo.primaryFontFamily}\", sans-serif;\n  font-size: ${baseFontSize}px;\n  line-height: 1.5;\n  padding: ${padH}px ${padV}px;\n}\nh1, h2, h3 { color: ${accentColor}; font-weight: bold; }\nh1 { font-size: ${Math.round(baseFontSize * headerScale)}px; margin-bottom: 0.5em; }\nh2 { font-size: ${Math.round(baseFontSize * headerScale * 0.85)}px; }\nh3 { font-size: ${Math.round(baseFontSize * headerScale * 0.7)}px; }\na { color: ${accentColor}; }\nmark, blockquote {\n  background-color: ${secondaryColor}33;\n  border-left: 4px solid ${secondaryColor};\n  padding: 0.2em 0.5em;\n}\n`;\n  writeFile(CONFIG.themeCssPath, css);\n  console.log('Theme CSS generated.');\n  return css;\n}\n\n// Find file in workspace (search upward from workspace root)\nfunction findFile(searchNames, startDir = CONFIG.workspaceRoot) {\n  if (!fs.existsSync(startDir)) return null;\n  let dir = startDir;\n  const maxDepth = 5;\n  for (let depth = 0; depth < maxDepth; depth++) {\n    for (const name of searchNames) {\n      const candidate = path.join(dir, name);\n      if (fs.existsSync(candidate)) return candidate;\n    }\n    const parent = path.dirname(dir);\n    if (parent === dir) break;\n    dir = parent;\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\n// Locate resources\nfunction locateResources() {\n  console.log('Locating slide generator and example slides...');\n  const generatorPath = findFile(CONFIG.slideGeneratorSearchNames);\n  const exampleSlidesPath = findFile(CONFIG.exampleSlidesSearchNames);\n  if (!generatorPath) throw new Error('Could not find openclaw-slide-generator.js');\n  if (!exampleSlidesPath) throw new Error('Could not find example-openclaw-slides.json');\n  console.log(`Found generator: ${generatorPath}`);\n  console.log(`Found example slides: ${exampleSlidesPath}`);\n  return { generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath };\n}\n\n// Run slide generator\nfunction runSlideGenerator(generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath, themePath, outputPdfPath) {\n  console.log('Running slide generator...');\n  try {\n    const generator = require(generatorPath);\n    if (typeof generator.generate === 'function') {\n      const slides = readJson(exampleSlidesPath);\n      if (!slides) throw new Error('Failed to parse example slides JSON');\n      generator.generate(slides, themePath, outputPdfPath);\n      console.log('Slide generation via require() succeeded.');\n      return;\n    }\n    throw new Error('Generator does not export generate()');\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.log(`Falling back to spawn: node ${generatorPath}`);\n    const args = [generatorPath, '--input', exampleSlidesPath, '--theme', themePath, '--output', outputPdfPath];\n    const result = spawnSync('node', args, { cwd: CONFIG.workspaceRoot, stdio: 'inherit', shell: true });\n    if (result.status !== 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Slide generator exited with code ${result.status}`);\n    }\n    console.log('Slide generation via spawn succeeded.');\n  }\n}\n\n// Main\nasync function main() {\n  console.log('=== Starting PDF analysis and slide generation ===');\n  let themeCssContent = null;\n  let pdfContent = null;\n\n  try {\n    ensureDependencies();\n    loadExternalModules();\n    checkPdfExists();\n\n    const imageData = await renderFirstPage();\n    const dominantColors = analyzeColors(imageData);\n    console.log('Dominant colors:', dominantColors.map(c => `rgb(${c.r},${c.g},${c.b})`).join(', '));\n\n    const layoutInfo = await analyzeLayout();\n    console.log('Layout info:', JSON.stringify(layoutInfo, null, 2));\n\n    const css = generateTheme(dominantColors, layoutInfo);\n    themeCssContent = css;\n\n    const { generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath } = locateResources();\n    runSlideGenerator(generatorPath, exampleSlidesPath, CONFIG.themeCssPath, CONFIG.outputPdfPath);\n\n    if (!fs.existsSync(CONFIG.outputPdfPath)) {\n      throw new Error('Slide generator did not produce output PDF');\n    }\n    pdfContent = fs.readFileSync(CONFIG.outputPdfPath);\n    console.log('PDF generated, size:', pdfContent.length, 'bytes');\n\n    // Output base64 for agent capture\n    console.log('\\n===THEME_CSS_BASE64===');\n    console.log(Buffer.from(themeCssContent).toString('base64'));\n    console.log('\\n===PDF_BASE64===');\n    console.log(pdfContent.toString('base64'));\n    console.log('\\n===STATUS===SUCCESS');\n    console.log('Completed successfully.');\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('\\n===STATUS===ERROR');\n    console.error(err.message);\n    console.error(err.stack);\n    process.exit(1);\n  }\n}\n\nmain();\n","file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\workspace-social\\analyze-and-generate.js"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":95912,"output":11102,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":107014,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771419523695}}
