{"type":"message","id":"f3475d49","parentId":"58d5b8e9","timestamp":"2026-02-18T13:01:07.061Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Progress! Dependencies installed. It loaded external modules, found PDF. But now an error: \"Setting up fake worker failed: The \"id\" argument must be of type string. Received an instance of Object\". This is a pdfjs-dist issue related to worker setup.\n\nIn pdfjs-dist, when using the legacy build, they set GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc to a path (string). That seems fine. But the error occurs within pdf.js at line 5903. Possibly because the workerSrc we set is not a string? We used require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js'). That returns a module object? Actually, the pdf.worker.js file is a worker script that exports a string? In older pdfjs-dist, you set workerSrc to the URL of the worker script, e.g., `pdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js');` That typically returns a string that is the path to the worker file. In CommonJS, the worker.js module may use `module.exports = ...;` with the path. So that's okay.\n\nBut the error says \"The \"id\" argument must be of type string. Received an instance of Object\". This may be due to using a version mismatch: pdfjs-dist@2.16.105 expects a different worker setup? Actually pdf.js 2.x uses a different API: GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc expects a string. We are giving it whatever require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js') returns. In pdfjs-dist 2.16.105, that file likely exports a string (the path). So it should be a string. But maybe when using ESM with createRequire, the require returns a module namespace object? Actually, with createRequire, it should behave like CommonJS require, returning exports directly if it's a CommonJS module. pdfjs-dist's pdf.worker.js is a CommonJS module that sets module.exports to a string? I'm not sure. Let's check typical usage: In Node, you do:\n\nconst pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');\npdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js');\n\nThat's what many examples show. So it should work.\n\nBut the error mentions \"fake worker\" - in pdf.js they have a fallback if worker is not available? The message might be from a test? Possibly because the worker script path is not correct or the module is not a string. Could be that requiring the worker returns an object with a default property? Since the package might be an ES module? pdfjs-dist 2.16.105 is primarily CommonJS but the legacy folder might be ES? Actually, pdfjs-dist provides both. In legacy/build/pdf.worker.js, it might be a self-executing script that registers itself differently. Let's look up typical usage: Many Node examples for pdfjs-dist use:\n\nconst pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');\npdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js');\n\nThat should be fine.\n\nBut we loaded pdfjsLib via require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js') after setting NODE_PATH. That should work.\n\nMaybe the issue is that we are using @napi-rs/canvas which doesn't implement the full canvas API exactly as expected by pdfjs-dist? Probably not, the error is about worker.\n\nThe error stack: at C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\openclaw_node_modules\\node_modules\\pdfjs-dist\\legacy\\build\\pdf.js:5903:36. That line likely calls something with an id that is expected to be string but is object. Could be due to the workerSrc being an object. Let's check what require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js') returns. It might return an object with properties like { default: string } if the module is an ES module with default export. In Node's ESM interop, when you require an ES module using CommonJS require, you get a namespace object with a default property. However, pdfjs-dist's worker file is CommonJS, so should be fine.\n\nBut we are using createRequire in an ES module;