{"type":"message","id":"ca0013ae","parentId":"ae52ff42","timestamp":"2026-02-18T13:02:54.409Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Again, exits after \"PDF loaded: 12 pages.\" No error captured. The script didn't produce the error file? Not yet. Let's check if the error file exists in TEMP_DIR. TEMP_DIR is C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\Temp. I can try to read `analysis-error.txt` from there.\n\nBut before that, maybe the script crashed before reaching the catch because the error was thrown in a different tick (like an unhandled rejection from something else). But the only async we have before the try catch is inside main. Actually the try block starts at `ensureDependencies()` and ends at the entire main body. So any error in those awaited calls should be caught.\n\nCould it be that `renderFirstPage` threw an error that was not caught because it was thrown after an await that resumed on a different phase? No, try/catch covers async functions.\n\nWait, maybe the error occurs in the `page.getPage(1)` call, which is inside renderFirstPage, before the render. That is awaited in renderFirstPage, which is then awaited in main. So caught.\n\nMaybe the error occurs after \"PDF loaded\" but before we get to `await renderFirstPage()`? The line after loading PDF is `const imageData = await renderFirstPage();`. That's a direct next line. So if it threw, the catch would handle.\n\nBut we see no error. Could the process have exited without an exception, maybe due to calling process.exit() inadvertently? We call process.exit(1) in catch, but that is only after logging. That would produce error logs.\n\nPerhaps the script reached an infinite loop or deadlock? No, it exited quickly with code 1.\n\nMaybe the error occurs during the loadExternalModules step? But we saw \"External modules loaded.\" printed. That's after `workerPath` line. So loadExternalModules succeeded.\n\nMaybe the error is during `createCanvas(viewport.width, viewport.height)`. That could throw if dimensions are not finite or too large. The viewport dimensions of a PDF page could be huge if the PDF has a very large page size (like 5000x5000). But even then, canvas might throw. That would be caught.\n\nBut why no error output? Possibly because the error object is not printable? It would still print.\n\nMaybe the PTY output got cut off due to the terminal control sequences. The error might be there but we didn't see because the scroll happened. The `poll` output may only include the most recent lines after the cursor moved. We might need to capture the full output including lines that scrolled off. But the process tool likely returns the full accumulated output, not just visible. However, the presence of control sequences like `[?25l` etc suggests it's a terminal session and the output captured includes those sequences. The `poll` output we saw is the entire captured output as a string. It shows \"PDF loaded: 12 pages.\" and then a control sequence `]0;Administrator: ...` which is the title setting? Then the process exited. So no error.\n\nMaybe the script threw an exception that was caught, and then the catch called `process.exit(1)` after logging. The logging might have been to stderr, which we didn't see because the log output only captured stdout? Actually, our logging uses `console.log` for normal and `console.error` for errors. The PTY likely captures both stdout and stderr combined. We saw error messages in previous runs. So should see.\n\nBut we didn't see any `===STATUS===ERROR` line. So the catch didn't run. That suggests the error happened outside the try block. Could it be in the `loadExternalModules` function after `console.log` but before returning? Specifically, `require.resolve` might throw if the module is not found. But we saw \"External modules loaded.\" printed, so that succeeded.\n\nMaybe the error is in the `pdfjsLib.getDocument(CONFIG.pdfPath)` call. That is inside renderFirstPage. We have `console.log('Loading PDF...')` before it, then `const loadingTask = pdfjsLib.getDocument(CONFIG.pdfPath);` This returns a loading task immediately, not a promise yet. The `await loadingTask.promise` is where the actual loading happens. That is where the error might occur. If the PDF is corrupt or password-protected, the promise would reject. That would be caught.\n\nMaybe the error occurs when calling `page.getViewport`? That is after `await pdf.getPage(1)`. That could throw if page number invalid.\n\nBut we got \"PDF loaded: 12 pages.\" That indicates that `await loadingTask.promise` and `await pdf.getPage(1)` both succeeded, because we logged after `pdf.getPage(1)`? Actually, we log \"PDF loaded: ${pdf.numPages} pages.\" after `await loadingTask.promise`. Then we do `const page = await pdf.getPage(1);`. We didn't log after that. So \"PDF loaded: 12 pages.\" is after loading the document, not after getting the page. So the code hasn't yet called `page.getViewport`. So the error might be in `await pdf.getPage(1)` or later.\n\nIf `await pdf.getPage(1)` fails, it would throw and be caught. We would see error after that log, but we didn't.\n\nMaybe the error occurs after `pdf.getPage(1)` but before we log anything else. Our next line is `const viewport = page.getViewport({ scale: 1.0 });`. That could throw if the page object is invalid. But unlikely.\n\nMaybe the script got an unhandled rejection from the `page.render` because we didn't attach a catch to the promise? But we awaited. If the render promise rejects, it throws.\n\nGiven the mystery, maybe we should add try/catch around renderFirstPage call inside main to log specifically.\n\nBut the bigger issue: we are spending a lot of time debugging. Perhaps there is a more robust approach: Instead of writing a complex script, use the OpenClaw slide generator directly with a theme that matches the sample? But we need to generate the theme from the sample.\n\nMaybe we can skip the rendering step by using an alternative: Use `pdfjs-dist` to get the page's text content and also maybe get the background color from the page's `/Resources` dictionary? There might be a property like `page.pageCommon.background`? Not typical.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use a simpler approach: If the sample PDF is a static image slide (like a raster image inside PDF), we could extract that image directly using pdfjs-dist's `page.getImages()`? That would give us image data, and we could get dominant colors from that image without needing canvas? Actually, `page.getImages()` returns image objects with `width`, `height`, and `xdib`? It might give data in a format that requires canvas to decode? I think pdfjs-dist can extract image data as a raw buffer using `image.stream`? That might still require decoding.\n\nHmm.\n\nMaybe the issue is not with canvas but with pdfjs-dist's worker. We set workerSrc to a path, but maybe the worker file is not compatible with the way we are running (Node with N-API?). Or maybe we need to set `pdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.isWorkerDisabled = true;` to disable the worker? In Node, pdfjs-dist can use a fake worker implementation if you set that. Some examples show setting `GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = null;` and `GlobalWorkerOptions.isWorkerDisabled = true;` to avoid worker. Let's try that: disable worker.\n\nFrom pdfjs-dist docs for Node: You can set `GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = path.join(__dirname, 'pdf.worker.js');` or you can set `GlobalWorkerOptions.isWorkerDisabled = true;` to run in single-threaded mode. That might avoid the worker issue.\n\nOur error earlier before was about \"Setting up fake worker failed\", so maybe disabling worker and using the main thread would work. Let's try setting `pdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.isWorkerDisabled = true;` instead of workerSrc. That's simpler.\n\nRemove workerSrc setting and set isWorkerDisabled.\n\nIn loadExternalModules, after pdfjsLib = require(...), do:\n\npdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.isWorkerDisabled = true;\n\nLet's try that.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"The worker setup seems problematic. I'll disable the worker entirely since we're in a single-threaded environment:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3fde30e050da454ab45f1a52","name":"edit","arguments":{"file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\workspace-social\\analyze-and-generate.js","old_string":"function loadExternalModules() {\n  // Try to load canvas implementation: prefer @napi-rs/canvas, fallback to canvas\n  let canvasModule;\n  try {\n    canvasModule = require('@napi-rs/canvas');\n    console.log('Using @napi-rs/canvas.');\n  } catch (e) {\n    canvasModule = require('canvas');\n    console.log('Using canvas.');\n  }\n  createCanvas = canvasModule.createCanvas;\n  // pdfjs-dist\n  pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');\n  // Resolve worker script path as string\n  const workerPath = require.resolve('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.worker.js');\n  pdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = workerPath;\n  console.log('External modules loaded. Worker at:', workerPath);\n}","new_string":"function loadExternalModules() {\n  // Try to load canvas implementation: prefer @napi-rs/canvas, fallback to canvas\n  let canvasModule;\n  try {\n    canvasModule = require('@napi-rs/canvas');\n    console.log('Using @napi-rs/canvas.');\n  } catch (e) {\n    canvasModule = require('canvas');\n    console.log('Using canvas.');\n  }\n  createCanvas = canvasModule.createCanvas;\n  // pdfjs-dist\n  pdfjsLib = require('pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.js');\n  // Disable worker since we're in Node and have issues with worker setup\n  pdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.isWorkerDisabled = true;\n  console.log('External modules loaded. Worker disabled.');\n}"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":113989,"output":4191,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":118180,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":177