{"type":"message","id":"d5a9e929","parentId":"516af321","timestamp":"2026-02-18T13:03:24.783Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Same: exits after PDF loaded. No error. Something is causing the process to exit immediately after that line, without throwing an exception that we catch. Could it be that the script is calling `process.exit(0)` inadvertently? No.\n\nMaybe the script ran out of memory or hit an fatal error that caused Node to exit without throwing, like an assertion failure in native code? The @napi-rs/canvas might be causing a crash when we try to create a canvas with certain dimensions? That would cause a non-zero exit code, maybe 1? Node usually exits with code 134 on segfault, but could be 1.\n\nBut we didn't see a core dump.\n\nLet's try to simplify: comment out the renderFirstPage call and see if the script continues to the next steps. That would isolate if the crash is in renderFirstPage. I can't easily modify and run quickly.\n\nI'll create a minimal test: just after loading PDF, try to create a small canvas and get context, without rendering page. That might also crash if canvas works.\n\nLet's modify the script temporarily: In renderFirstPage, after creating canvas and ctx, we could try to fill a rectangle to test if canvas works, then return dummy data. That would help isolate if the crash is during page.render or during canvas creation or something else.\n\nBut we need to proceed quickly. However, we have time (up to 60 min). We can debug.\n\nGiven the script exits with code 1 after \"PDF loaded: 12 pages.\" and no error, I suspect an unhandled rejection in the `await renderFirstPage()` that kills the process before catch can log. But try/catch should catch it.\n\nMaybe the error occurs in the `await loadExternalModules()`? No, that was before.\n\nWait, the try block includes `ensureDependencies(); loadExternalModules(); checkPdfExists();` and then `const imageData = await renderFirstPage();`. If renderFirstPage throws, it should be caught. But what if renderFirstPage throws an error that is not a standard Error object, maybe a DOMException? Still catchable.\n\nMaybe the error is thrown in a microtask that is not awaited? For example, the `page.render(...).promise` returns a promise that, when rejected, will reject the await. That is caught.\n\nSo puzzling.\n\nPerhaps the script is actually continuing after renderFirstPage but the output is getting lost because the PTY output is being overwritten by a large amount of data from npm-install.log? No, npm install already completed.\n\nLet's try to capture more by setting a longer timeout and maybe not using pty? But we need pty for output.\n\nAlternatively, we could redirect the script's stdout to a file using shell redirection in the exec command, then read that file. For example: `node analyze-and-generate.js > output.txt 2>&1`. Then we can read output.txt. That might capture all output without PTY control sequences. But exec with pty gives us interactive terminal; without pty we can still redirect to file and then read the file. The file would be written on the gateway's filesystem. But we need to read it. If we write to a temp file path we know, we can read it.\n\nWe can modify the exec command to: `node analyze-and-generate.js > D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\workspace-social\\stdout.txt 2>&1`. But that's using D:\\... which may not be writable on gateway. But we can redirect to TEMP_DIR: `node analyze-and-generate.js > ${TEMP_DIR}\\\\script-output.txt 2>&1`. But we need to know TEMP_DIR path inside the command. We can construct in the command: `node analyze-and-generate.js > C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\script-output.txt 2>&1`. That should work.\n\nThen after the process exits, we can attempt to read that file. It might be on the gateway's filesystem and we might be able to read it using read tool if the gateway's TEMP is accessible. It should be, since it's the same user's temp.\n\nLet's try.\n\nWe'll run:\n\n```\ncmd /c \"node analyze-and-generate.js > C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\script-output.txt 2>&1\"\n```\n\nBut we need to use the gateway and pty or not? We can run without pty, because we're redirecting to a file. We can then read the file.\n\nBut the exec tool still needs to capture something? We'll still get a session. We'll poll and then read the file.\n\nLet's try:\n\nexec(host=\"gateway\", command='node analyze-and-generate.js > \"C:\\\\Users\\\\mangs\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Temp\\\\script-output.txt\" 2>&1', workdir=..., background=True, yieldMs=...)\n\nAfter it exits, read the file.\n\nBut we must ensure the command runs in a shell to interpret redirection. Use cmd /c or set shell option. We can use shell=true.\n\nLet's do:\n\n```\nnode analyze-and-generate.js > C:\\Users\\mangs\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\script-output.txt 2>&1\n```\n\nBut on Windows, the redirection operator > works in cmd. So we should run through cmd /c. Use shell=true: `exec(..., shell: true)`? The exec tool has a parameter? The tool description doesn't list a \"shell\" parameter. We have pty. But we can explicitly run `cmd /c` as part of the command.\n\nSo command: `cmd /c \"node analyze-and-generate.js > \\\"C:\\\\Users\\\\mangs\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Temp\\\\script-output.txt\\\" 2>&1\"`\n\nWe'll use double quotes around the whole thing and escape inner quotes.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe easier: Since we are already writing an error file, we can also write a success marker file. But we haven't gotten that far.\n\nMaybe we can add a try/catch around the entire renderFirstPage and see if it throws. Actually we already have. But we need to see the error.\n\nLet's manually insert a step after PDF loaded to log and then create a small canvas and draw something to test if canvas works. If that also fails, we'll see error.\n\nI'll modify the script to include a test after loading PDF, before renderFirstPage, to create a small canvas and fill it. That will isolate if canvas 